WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:03.110
What do you want me to say?

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:07.631
Or, more importantly, how
do you want me to say it?

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.760
I could stand here and
narrate my world to you

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.360
like it's the opening
credits of a disaster movie.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.280
This is the setup.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.770
2050, far enough in the future
for all this to seem plausible.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.280
Climate catastrophe reported
as normality, the daily index

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.590
of maximum time
allowed outside in Doha

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.540
or Austin or Manchester.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.840
In all these places, it is
over 50 degrees centigrade.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.180
If you go outside for more
than your allotted hour,

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.840
then you will die.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.270
Some specific new tragedy.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.160
Targeted bioweapon
takes out Cape Town

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.790
as the statelets of a collapsed
South Africa fight for water.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.940
First use of live ammunition
to keep the hordes out

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.380
on the Italian/Swiss border.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.310
I could be a newsreader,
if we still have those.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.730
A citizen journalist,
screaming into a phone

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.660
against the backdrop of
burning old growth forest.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.100
Ported into your optic nerve
through a neural network,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.290
a studio improvised
in a spare bedroom

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.850
to half a billion subscribers.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.260
But not anything we'd recognise
as TV news, or newspapers,

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:22.580
obviously.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.280
Whatever a newspaper
looks like then,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.640
we're probably
imagining it wrong.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.400
Whatever happens,
whatever lies look like,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.320
whatever truth becomes, it
probably won't look like this.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.030
This isn't really the future.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.510
This is an idea of the
future that by the time

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.770
you're watching it is
in the past anyway.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:54.480
This is what the future looked
like in our heads in 2019.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.960
I'm just here saying, how do
you want me to talk to us?

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.610
Should we talk about
dystopia, maybe?

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.440
But dystopia is weird, isn't it?

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.690
It feels now like we might
have made a mistake about it.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.130
Depending on which version
of this future world

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:16.060
I'm in right now, of course.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.520
I mean, maybe we scared
ourselves a bit too much.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.570
All that footage
of rising oceans,

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.320
and rioting in the
cities, and the streets

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.260
of London underwater,
and one filthy

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:31.670
polar bear that was all
ribs, and hunger, and sadness.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.130
All those water
wars, desiccated dead

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.860
piled in the uninhabitable
streets, and the rise of idiots

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.840
feeding on fear with
simplistic solutions.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.840
A lot of that happened.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.910
Or from where you're
watching this, a lot of that

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.310
is going to happen.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.750
The processes are in motion.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.230
Dengue fever's gearing
up for a world tour.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.140
Extreme heat's
eyeing up more people

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.970
than just the old
or young or sick.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.040
People are dying of
this stuff right now.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:09.020
But if that's what
we tell ourselves,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.130
we internalise the
end, the idea of it.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.830
We lie down in the face of
it because it's just too big,

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:18.500
and then that is the end.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.040
Because we'll put our trust, our
need to be saved from all that,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.780
in things we can't see yet.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.450
We'll take the chance of an
off-moment of inspiration

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.610
over the provable reality.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.800
It's just the way we're built.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.400
The big discoveries of
self-regenerating bodies,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.490
and fusion power, and
everything quiet, and humming,

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.070
and electric.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.570
Bringing the global
temperature down

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.700
with sulphates or electric cars
or some method of clean energy

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.240
we haven't even thought of yet.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.720
Our ability to
pull salvation out

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.040
of the apocalypse hat at
the last minute, to carbon

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.940
capture our way out of this.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.560
The idea there's a
sole solution that

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:09.610
will save us through the
power of eureka is seductive.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.133
And we're trying
all these things.

00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:14.440
We are.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:20.839
But whatever
arrives, if it does,

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:25.010
it can't reach back
through time to now.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.830
We've never really worked
out how to talk about this.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.210
We probably should have.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.793
I mean, it's not as
if we didn't know.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.220
Even if some of us took
comfort in the voices

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.808
that only told us what
we wanted to hear.

00:04:39.808 --> 00:04:42.100
And then we spent a long time
trying to blind ourselves

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.542
with science, and we're sorry.

00:04:46.542 --> 00:04:47.750
That didn't necessarily work.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.440
Graphs.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.230
Graphs, red lines, colours,
and concentrations,

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:58.070
parts per million, and rises
of degrees, and millimetres.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.980
And we look around ourselves
and think, don't we?

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:08.330
These numbers are too small
or too large or meaningless.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:13.320
What does 2 degrees
mean on a cold day?

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.550
And what does it mean
decades from now?

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:20.110
Which of them is easier
to feel in our bones

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:23.380
and then get on with our lives?

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:27.280
If we could have sung
scientific facts like pop songs,

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.500
we'd have got to the chorus
sooner, but we didn't.

00:05:33.322 --> 00:05:34.780
It's not that we
could have stopped

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.600
these things from happening.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.730
But we'd have been better
prepared to face them

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.560
in a way that didn't
kill so many of us.

00:05:48.128 --> 00:05:49.920
Maybe we screwed
ourselves up with too much

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.350
speculative fiction.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:55.100
I could be standing
at the edge of the sea

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.780
telling you the future
looks like this,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.880
that most of the
streets you grew up in

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.920
are behind me in the waves.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.370
List the countries
that are gone,

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.920
and the parts of countries,
because they're underwater

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.270
or you just can't
go outside in them,

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:13.010
where the local
biospheres collapsed,

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.423
show you the normal
life that plays out

00:06:15.423 --> 00:06:16.340
against that backdrop.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.070
A kind of warmer, more
beige version of now

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:23.600
with potholes and soldiers
on the streets everywhere,

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.210
rather than just
the poor places.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.830
And only three
types of toothpaste.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:36.820
Thing is, when you make the
argument about lifestyle

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.520
degrading, you either
don't want to believe it

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.935
because you think you're
immune, or you don't care,

00:06:43.935 --> 00:06:45.310
because you're
already living it.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:54.310
But the one thing nobody
told us was that everything

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.080
was going to be all right.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.440
Nobody serious, anyway.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:02.710
And if that's what you heard,
it wasn't what we were saying.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:08.710
We never found the way to
make ourselves do enough,

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.930
fast enough.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:14.120
I mean, some of us, yes.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.720
But doing enough
consistently, talking

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.150
in a way that made
this possible to grasp

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:24.470
in the quiet parts of life in
the times between the times

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:29.700
that we all march, no.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.740
We needed to make this
as everyday as bath time.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.990
As graspable as pre-packed
sandwiches or your loved

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.656
one's hand.

00:07:39.656 --> 00:07:42.510
And we didn't.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.960
Every time we tried to make
the defining statement,

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.660
we thought, maybe
this is the one.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.660
Maybe this is the one
that gets through.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:55.160
We didn't need data or heroes.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:56.840
We didn't even need
voices pretending

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.790
to be from the future.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.360
We're in the same place.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.950
It's not about our children
or our grandchildren anymore.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.350
The future has come to meet us.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:10.975
Most of the people watching this
will be there when it happens.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.190
We needed to
understand, quietly,

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:20.750
every day, that
this is the future--

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:22.180
now.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.520
Every single second.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.190
We're not waiting
for the future.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:30.730
We're not trying to fend it off.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:36.130
We're living in it.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:43.330
It is now.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.130
And it is us.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:51.180
 

